1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:22,160
How can I speak in ten minutes about the bonds of women over three generations?

2
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:27,000
About how the astonishing strength of those bonds took hold in the life of a four year

3
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:34,240
old girl, huddled with her young sister, her mother, and her grandmother, for five days

4
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:40,200
and nights in a small boat in the China Sea more than thirty years ago.

5
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:45,000
Bonds that took hold in the life of that small girl and never let go.

6
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,560
That small girl now living in San Francisco and speaking to you today.

7
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:56,600
This is not a finished story, it is a jigsaw puzzle still being put together.

8
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:00,680
Let me tell you about some of the pieces.

9
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:05,120
Imagine the first piece, a man burning his life's work.

10
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:11,640
He is a poet, a playwright, a man whose whole life had been balanced on the single hope

11
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:15,060
of his country's unity and freedom.

12
00:01:15,060 --> 00:01:19,800
Imagine him as the communists enter Saigon, confronting the fact that his life had been

13
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:21,920
a complete waste.

14
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:25,680
Words for so long his friends now mocked him.

15
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:28,160
He retreated into silence.

16
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:31,920
He died broken by history.

17
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:34,440
He is my grandfather.

18
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:38,480
I never knew him in real life.

19
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:41,860
But our lives are much more than our memories.

20
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:45,120
My grandmother never let me forget his life.

21
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:50,640
My duty was not to allow it to have been in vain, and my lesson was to learn that yes,

22
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:55,440
history tried to crush us, but we endured.

23
00:01:55,440 --> 00:02:00,440
The next piece of the jigsaw is of a boat in the early dawn, slipping silently out to

24
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:02,280
sea.

25
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:08,420
My mother Mai was 18 when her father died, already in an arranged marriage, already with

26
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:10,980
two small girls.

27
00:02:10,980 --> 00:02:16,600
For her, life had distilled itself into one task, the escape of her family and a new life

28
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:18,960
in Australia.

29
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:23,160
It was inconceivable to her that she would not succeed.

30
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:28,240
So after a four-year saga that defies fiction, a boat slipped out to sea, disguised as a

31
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:30,860
fishing vessel.

32
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:33,800
All the adults knew the risks.

33
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:38,480
The greatest fear was of pirates, rape and death.

34
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:43,920
Like most adults on the boat, my mother carried a small bottle of poison.

35
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:50,900
If we were captured, first my sister and I, then she and my grandmother would drink.

36
00:02:50,900 --> 00:02:53,700
My first memories are from the boat.

37
00:02:53,700 --> 00:03:01,420
The steady beat of the engine, the bow dipping into each wave, the vast and empty horizon.

38
00:03:01,420 --> 00:03:05,860
I don't remember the pirates who came many times but were bluffed by the bravado of the

39
00:03:05,860 --> 00:03:12,700
men on our boat, or the engine dying and failing to start for six hours.

40
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:17,880
But I do remember the lights on the oil rig off the Malaysian coast, and the young man

41
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:23,560
who collapsed and died, the journey's end too much for him.

42
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:28,040
And the first apple I tasted, given to me by the men on the rig.

43
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:33,800
No apple has ever tasted the same.

44
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:38,880
After three months in a refugee camp, we landed in Melbourne, and the next piece of the jigsaw

45
00:03:38,880 --> 00:03:45,560
is about four women across three generations, shaping a new life together.

46
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:52,000
We settled in Footscray, a working class suburb whose demographic is layers of immigrants.

47
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,560
Unlike the settled middle class suburbs whose existence I was oblivious of, there was no

48
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:59,340
sense of entitlement in Footscray.

49
00:03:59,340 --> 00:04:03,680
The smells from shop doors were from the rest of the world, and the snippets of halting

50
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:07,900
English were exchanged between people who had one thing in common.

51
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:10,880
They were starting again.

52
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:17,720
My mother worked on farms, then on a car assembly line, working six days, double shifts.

53
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:22,680
Somehow she found time to study English and gain IT qualifications.

54
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:24,800
We were poor.

55
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:29,320
All the dollars were allocated, and extra tuition in English and mathematics was budgeted

56
00:04:29,320 --> 00:04:35,560
for, regardless of what missed out, which was usually new clothes.

57
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:37,800
They were always second hand.

58
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:42,320
Two pairs of stockings for school, each to hide the holes in the other.

59
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:48,300
A school uniform down to the ankles because it had to last for six years.

60
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:54,160
And there were rare but searing chants of slirai and the occasional graffiti, Asian

61
00:04:54,160 --> 00:04:56,160
go home.

62
00:04:56,160 --> 00:04:58,680
Go home to where?

63
00:04:58,680 --> 00:05:00,840
Something stiffened inside me.

64
00:05:00,840 --> 00:05:07,440
There was a gathering of resolve and a quiet voice saying, I will bypass you.

65
00:05:07,440 --> 00:05:12,080
My mother, my sister and I slept in the same bed.

66
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:16,740
My mother was exhausted each night, but we told one another about our day and listened

67
00:05:16,740 --> 00:05:20,600
to the movements of my grandmother around the house.

68
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:24,960
My mother suffered from nightmares, all about the boat.

69
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:31,560
And my job was to stay awake until her nightmares came so I could wake her.

70
00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:36,960
She opened a computer store, then studied to be a beautician and opened another business.

71
00:05:36,960 --> 00:05:41,680
And the women came with their stories about men who could not make the transition, angry

72
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:46,600
and inflexible and troubled children caught between two worlds.

73
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:51,320
Grants and sponsors were sought, centres were established.

74
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:53,540
I lived in parallel worlds.

75
00:05:53,540 --> 00:06:00,040
In one, I was the classic Asian student, relentless in the demands I made on myself.

76
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:05,060
In the other, I was enmeshed in lives that were precarious, tragically scarred by violence,

77
00:06:05,140 --> 00:06:08,140
drug abuse and isolation.

78
00:06:08,140 --> 00:06:10,220
But so many over the years were helped.

79
00:06:10,220 --> 00:06:14,740
And for that work, when I was a final year law student, I was chosen as the Young Australian

80
00:06:14,740 --> 00:06:15,740
of the Year.

81
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:19,760
And I was catapulted from one piece of the jigsaw to another.

82
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:22,260
And their edges didn't fit.

83
00:06:22,260 --> 00:06:28,260
Tarnley, anonymous Footscray resident, was now Tarnley refugee and social activist, invited

84
00:06:28,260 --> 00:06:33,780
to speak in venues she had never heard of and into homes whose existence she could never

85
00:06:33,780 --> 00:06:35,680
have imagined.

86
00:06:35,680 --> 00:06:37,760
I didn't know the protocols.

87
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:40,500
I didn't know how to use the cutlery.

88
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:43,220
I didn't know how to talk about wine.

89
00:06:43,220 --> 00:06:46,820
I didn't know how to talk about anything.

90
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:53,700
I wanted to retreat to the routines and comfort of life in an unsung suburb, a grandmother,

91
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:59,620
a mother and two daughters, ending each day as they had for almost 20 years, telling one

92
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:04,820
another the story of their day and falling asleep, the three of us still in the same

93
00:07:04,820 --> 00:07:07,540
bed.

94
00:07:07,540 --> 00:07:11,260
I told my mother I couldn't do it.

95
00:07:11,260 --> 00:07:17,060
She reminded me that I was now the same age she had been when we boarded the boat.

96
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:20,460
No had never been an option.

97
00:07:20,460 --> 00:07:24,660
Just do it, she said, and don't be what you're not.

98
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:29,540
So I spoke out on youth unemployment and education and the neglect of the marginalised and

99
00:07:29,580 --> 00:07:36,620
disenfranchised, and the more candidly I spoke, the more I was asked to speak.

100
00:07:36,620 --> 00:07:41,540
I met people from all walks of life, so many of them doing the thing they loved, living

101
00:07:41,540 --> 00:07:44,060
on the frontiers of possibility.

102
00:07:44,060 --> 00:07:49,620
And even though I finished my degree, I realised I could not settle into a career in law.

103
00:07:49,620 --> 00:07:53,400
There had to be another piece of the jigsaw.

104
00:07:53,400 --> 00:08:00,240
And I realised at the same time that it is okay to be an outsider, a recent arrival,

105
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:07,200
new on the scene, and not just okay, but something to be thankful for, perhaps a gift from the

106
00:08:07,200 --> 00:08:14,080
boat, because being an insider can so easily mean collapsing the horizons, can so easily

107
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:18,320
mean accepting the presumptions of your province.

108
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:22,560
I had stepped outside my comfort zone enough now to know that yes, the world does fall

109
00:08:22,600 --> 00:08:26,000
apart, but not in the way that you fear.

110
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:30,480
Possibilities that would not have been allowed were outrageously encouraged.

111
00:08:30,480 --> 00:08:37,800
There was an energy there, an implacable optimism, a strange mixture of humility and daring.

112
00:08:37,800 --> 00:08:39,400
So I followed my hunches.

113
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:44,040
I gathered around me a small team of people for whom the label, it can't be done, was

114
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:46,840
an irresistible challenge.

115
00:08:46,840 --> 00:08:48,880
For a year we were penniless.

116
00:08:48,880 --> 00:08:53,360
At the end of each day I made a huge pot of soup which we all shared.

117
00:08:53,360 --> 00:08:56,000
We worked well into each night.

118
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:02,920
Most of our ideas were crazy, but a few were brilliant and we broke through.

119
00:09:02,920 --> 00:09:09,200
I made the decision to move to the US after only one trip, my hunches again.

120
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:15,320
Three months later I had relocated and the adventure has continued.

121
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:19,520
Before I close though, let me tell you about my grandmother.

122
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:24,200
She grew up at a time when Confucianism was the social norm and the local Mandarin was

123
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:26,560
the person who mattered.

124
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:29,560
Life hadn't changed for centuries.

125
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:33,160
Her father died soon after she was born.

126
00:09:33,160 --> 00:09:36,160
Her mother raised her alone.

127
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:42,220
At 17 she became the second wife of a Mandarin whose mother beat her.

128
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:47,600
With no support from her husband, she caused a sensation by taking him to court and prosecuting

129
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:58,500
her own case, and a far greater sensation when she won.

130
00:09:58,500 --> 00:10:03,980
It can't be done was shown to be wrong.

131
00:10:03,980 --> 00:10:12,480
I was taking a shower in a hotel room in Sydney the moment she died, 600 miles away in Melbourne.

132
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:16,600
I looked through the shower screen and saw her standing on the other side.

133
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:19,400
I knew she had come to say goodbye.

134
00:10:19,400 --> 00:10:22,680
My mother phoned minutes later.

135
00:10:22,680 --> 00:10:28,400
A few days later we went to a Buddhist temple in Footscray and sat around her casket.

136
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:33,200
We told her stories and assured her that we were still with her.

137
00:10:33,200 --> 00:10:38,860
At midnight, the monk came and told us he had to close the casket.

138
00:10:38,860 --> 00:10:42,060
My mother asked us to feel her hand.

139
00:10:42,060 --> 00:10:49,260
She asked the monk, why is it that her hand is so warm and the rest of her is so cold?

140
00:10:49,260 --> 00:10:53,120
Because you have been holding it since this morning, he said.

141
00:10:53,120 --> 00:10:57,720
You have not let it go.

142
00:10:57,720 --> 00:11:02,680
If there is a sinew in our family, it runs through the women.

143
00:11:02,680 --> 00:11:07,260
Given who we were and how life had shaped us, we can now see that the men that might

144
00:11:07,260 --> 00:11:10,820
have come into our lives would have thwarted us.

145
00:11:10,820 --> 00:11:14,080
Defeat would have come too easily.

146
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:19,240
Now I would like to have my own children and I wonder about the boat.

147
00:11:19,240 --> 00:11:22,500
Who could ever wish it on their own?

148
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:27,980
Yet I am afraid of privilege, of ease, of entitlement.

149
00:11:27,980 --> 00:11:32,960
Can I give them a bow in their lives, dipping bravely into each wave?

150
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:40,800
The unperturbed and steady beat of the engine, the vast horizon that guarantees nothing.

151
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:46,800
I don't know, but if I could give it and still see them safely through, I would.

152
00:11:57,980 --> 00:12:05,360
And also, Han's mother is here today in the fourth of February.

